## Proof

We claim that such a set $B$ always exists.

Since $f:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}$ is measurable and real-valued (hence finite at every point), define for each $n\in\mathbb{N}$ the set
$$E_n = \{x\in[0,1] : |f(x)|\le n\}.$$

Each $E_n$ is measurable (it is the preimage of the closed interval $[-n,n]$ under the measurable function $|f|$). The sequence $(E_n)$ is increasing: $E_1\subseteq E_2\subseteq\cdots$.

Because $f$ is finite-valued everywhere, for every $x\in[0,1]$ there exists some $n$ with $|f(x)|\le n$. Hence
$$\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} E_n = [0,1].$$

By continuity of measure from below,
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\mu(E_n) = \mu\!\left(\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty}E_n\right) = \mu([0,1]) = 1.$$

Since $\mu(E_n)\to 1 > 0$, there exists some $N$ with $\mu(E_N)>0$. Set $B = E_N$. Then $B$ is measurable, $\mu(B)>0$, and for every $x\in B$ we have $|f(x)|\le N$, so $f$ is bounded on $B$.

Therefore such a set $B$ always exists. $\blacksquare$

